### 🔴 New critical issue found during the re-audit

`blog_posts` had a policy named **"Service can manage blog"** granted to
**public** with `USING (true)` and command `ALL`. The name implied it was
scoped to the service role — it was not. Anyone with the public anon key
(which is necessarily in your frontend source) could insert, edit or
delete any blog post: defacement, SEO spam injection, or wiping the blog.

The first audit missed this because I checked `reviews` specifically and
read the policy *names* elsewhere rather than their actual clauses. Fixed
— policy dropped. The backend uses the service-role key, which bypasses
RLS by design, so nothing legitimate needed it.

This is exactly why the re-audit was worth doing.

